  'hemagglutinin' => 'A surface glycoprotein, especially on influenza viruses, that binds sialic-acid receptors and helps the virus attach to and enter host cells; it is a major target of protective antibodies.',
  'hemangioendothelioma' => 'A vascular neoplasm arising from endothelial cells with malignant potential between benign hemangioma and high-grade angiosarcoma; behavior depends on subtype and site.',
  'hemangiopericytoma' => 'A rare tumor formerly defined by branching “staghorn” vessels and perivascular cells; many lesions classified this way are now recognized as solitary fibrous tumors.',
  'hematocolpos' => 'Accumulation of menstrual blood in the vagina, usually because an imperforate hymen or another obstructing anomaly prevents outflow; it may cause cyclic pelvic pain and a bulging vaginal membrane.',
  'hemianopsia' => 'Loss of vision in half of the visual field of one or both eyes, usually from a lesion of the optic tract, radiations, or occipital cortex; sudden onset may indicate stroke.',
  'hemidesmosome' => 'A specialized junction that anchors basal epithelial cells to the underlying basement membrane by linking intracellular keratin filaments with extracellular matrix proteins.',
  'hemifacial atrophy' => 'Progressive wasting of skin, subcutaneous tissue, muscle, and sometimes bone on one side of the face, also called Parry--Romberg syndrome.',
  'hemihypertrophy' => 'Unequal overgrowth of one side or region of the body, producing asymmetry of limbs, trunk, or face; some childhood cases carry increased risk of embryonal tumors.',
  'hemimegalencephaly' => 'A rare developmental brain malformation in which one cerebral hemisphere is abnormally enlarged and dysplastic, often causing infantile seizures, developmental impairment, and contralateral neurologic signs.',
  'hemimelia' => 'A congenital limb-reduction defect in which part or all of a limb segment is absent, such as fibular or radial hemimelia.',
  'hemocytometer' => 'A ruled counting chamber used with a microscope to determine the concentration of cells or other particles in a measured volume of fluid.',
  'hemodialysis clearance' => 'The volume of plasma from which hemodialysis removes a solute per unit time; it depends on blood and dialysate flow, membrane properties, treatment time, and the substance’s distribution.',
